<div class="main-text" style="text-align: justify;">
    <p><b><i><h1>Potato</h1></b><br>

    Potatoes are one of the most widely cultivated crops in the UK, growing well in <strong>most regions</strong>. 
    They are highly adaptable but perform best in <strong>mild, cool climates</strong>, making them suitable for 
    <strong>England, Scotland, Wales, and Northern Ireland</strong>. While potatoes can be grown in open fields, 
    gardeners in colder or wetter areas may benefit from <strong>raised beds</strong> or <strong>polytunnels</strong> 
    to improve drainage and prevent disease.<br><br>

    <b><h2>Best Growing Conditions</h2></b><br>
    - <strong>Climate:</strong> Cool to mild temperatures (10–20°C) with moderate rainfall.<br>
    - <strong>Soil:</strong> Well-drained, fertile soil with a pH between 5.0 and 6.0.<br>
    - <strong>Sunlight:</strong> At least 6 hours of direct sunlight daily.<br>
    - <strong>Watering:</strong> Keep the soil consistently moist but avoid waterlogging.<br>
    - <strong>Spacing:</strong> Rows should be spaced around 60 cm apart, with 30 cm between each plant.<br><br>

    <b><h2>Common Potato Diseases in the UK</h2></b><br>

    - <strong>Early Blight:</strong>  
      - <strong>Symptoms:</strong> Small brown spots with concentric rings on older leaves, leading to defoliation.<br>
      - <strong>Prevention & Treatment:</strong>  
        - Rotate crops to avoid soil-borne infections.<br>
        - Remove infected leaves and apply copper-based fungicides.<br>
        - Avoid overhead watering to keep foliage dry.<br><br>

    - <strong>Late Blight:</strong>  
      - <strong>Symptoms:</strong> Dark, water-soaked lesions on leaves and stems, rapidly spreading and destroying plants.<br>
      - <strong>Prevention & Treatment:</strong>  
        - Choose blight-resistant varieties such as ‘Sarpo Mira’.<br>
        - Harvest potatoes before wet and humid conditions encourage blight.<br>
        - Remove and destroy infected plants immediately to prevent spread.<br><br>

    <b><h2>Tips for Growing Potatoes in the UK</h2></b><br>
    - <strong>Chitting:</strong> Pre-sprouting seed potatoes indoors before planting speeds up growth.<br>
    - <strong>Earthing Up:</strong> Cover emerging shoots with soil to protect them from frost and encourage tuber growth.<br>
    - <strong>Crop Rotation:</strong> Avoid planting potatoes in the same spot each year to reduce disease risks.<br>
    - <strong>Harvest Timing:</strong> First early potatoes can be harvested in June, while maincrop varieties mature by September.<br>
    - <strong>Storage:</strong> Keep harvested potatoes in a cool, dark place to prevent sprouting and spoilage.<br><br>

    With the right care and attention, potatoes can be a reliable and productive crop across the UK, offering a steady supply of homegrown produce.
    </i></p>
